Stop two harnesses reporting a port conflict as a fault in the system under test (#143, #131) (#144)

* fix(smoke): OPS-004 picks its own Rekor port and verifies who answers it (#131)

TestOPS004FreshCloneBootstrap hardcoded rekorURL to 127.0.0.1:3000 even
though deploy/compose/sigstore.yml already parameterises the binding via
INNSEGL_REKOR_PORT. On this machine, host port 3000 was held by an
unrelated juice-authz container, and OPS-004 spent 289s reporting the
resulting HTTP 500 as a fault in our own Rekor.

Reproduced live: curl 127.0.0.1:3000/api/v1/log/publicKey returned
Juice Shop's "Unexpected path" 500 page, and the unmodified test failed
with that response wrapped as a rekor error.

Fix: the stack now picks a free host port right before boot and passes
it through INNSEGL_REKOR_PORT (stack.rekorPort/rekorURL), and
assertRekorPortIsOurs asks Docker's own port-publish table who is bound
to that port before any HTTP probe is allowed to run, failing fast and
naming the occupying container if it isn't innsegl-sigstore-rekor. Picking
the port late keeps the #143 probe-then-bind race window small without
touching the shared port helper.

New tests: OPS-014 (rekorPortConflict's branches from docker ps output,
no container needed) and OPS-015 (a real decoy container squatting on a
chosen port, proving assertRekorPortIsOurs names it).

* fix(chaos): survive the port-allocation race in OPS-001's bring-up (#143)

The harness picked a free ephemeral port, released it, then let Docker bind
it seconds to minutes later, leaving a window in which anything else on the
machine could take it first. Ports were reserved for the whole world up
front, which is why Sigstore's (used last, after SPIRE) had the widest
window and is what CI hit: "Bind for 127.0.0.1:33971 failed: port is already
allocated".

Reproduced deterministically before writing the fix: held a loopback port
with this process's own listener and asked Docker to publish a real
container onto it. Docker refused with the same message class the CI log
carries, confirmed against both an older and a newer Docker Engine's
wording. That reproduction also answers the issue's first question: a
listener held by a separate process blocks Docker's own bind, so "hold the
port until Docker takes it over" is not achievable across process
boundaries — Docker needs the port free at the instant it binds.

So each bring-up step (SPIRE, the admin bridge, Sigstore, Postgres, MinIO)
now picks its own port immediately before the docker call that needs it,
and retries with a fresh port, up to 5 times, ONLY when the daemon reports
that specific, distinguishable error — never on a genuine fault, which
still fails on the first attempt per #101. Attempts are logged so a machine
that needed several is visible. A docker-run step force-removes any
container a failed attempt left behind before retrying, since Docker leaves
that container in "Created" state and a bare retry would collide on the
name instead of losing the actual race. prtFreePort also now prefers a
20000-29999 band outside the kernel's own ephemeral range, narrowing the
window independently of the retry.

The "Found orphan containers" warning in the same CI log is unrelated: this
harness always brings up SPIRE's compose files before Sigstore's under the
same project name, so compose reports SPIRE's own just-started containers as
orphans relative to the Sigstore-only file set it was just given. It is
routine, not evidence of a half-populated project from a prior failure, and
is not touched here.

Test catalog IDs RM-092-1/2/3 (not doc 07 — this is harness plumbing, not an
OPS-001 product case): the classifier, the retry driver's bookkeeping, and
the real-Docker reproduction above, now kept as a permanent regression test.

Verified: 3 full real bring-ups of TestOPS001DependencyPartitionMatrix
